# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in the values you need. TabFleet loads .env.local on launch
# (before any module reads process.env). Shell env vars still win over this file.

# ─── TextChest ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A profile uses TextChest when it has a Site:X column with "smscode=textchest:API-KEY[:parser]"
# and an "SMS Number" column. The API key goes in the Authorization: Bearer <key> header; the
# number is normalised to digits only. Default template (overridden here only if TextChest changes
# the route — the base URL and path are from TextChest's own Developer docs):
# TABFLEET_TEXTCHEST_URL=https://api.textchest.com/{endpoint}?recipient={number}
#
# Placeholders substituted at fetch time:
#   {endpoint} — "messages" for textchest, "async_messages" for textchest-async
#   {number}   — the profile's SMS Number, digits only
#   {parser}   — the parser argument (defaults to "ticketmaster")
#   {key}      — rarely useful (key goes in the header), but available if your dashboard says so

# ─── Automatiq ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# For "password=automatiq:API-TOKEN:COMPANY-ID[:SITE-ID]" resolvers.
# Placeholders: {token}, {email}, {company}, {site}. The Authorization header is sent as
# "Bearer <token>" regardless of the URL template.
# TABFLEET_AUTOMATIQ_URL=https://api.automatiq.com/v1/accounts?email={email}&company_id={company}&site_id={site}
